eck happens before the type validation, so `mknod foo x` never reaches the code that would catch the invalid device type. Instead, it hits the missing operand check first and returns that error message. I need to reorder the validation so the type check happens before the operand count check, or restructure the logic to validate the device type early and produce the correct error message that matches GNU's behavior. Reordering the validation so type is checked first, before checking argument counts. This way invalid types get a plain error message without the usage hint, while missing arguments still trigger the full usage error. Fixing error message formatting...\n\nNow I'm thinking through what test cases are missing from the test suite. I should add tests for permission denied scenarios when trying to create a FIFO in a read-only directory, and also cover edge cases like `--mode` and `-m` flags without arguments to ensure they match GNU behavior.\n\nLooking at the existing `bad_type` test with arguments `[\"foo\", \"x\"]`, after my error message fix both binaries should now produce matching output, so that test should pass. I need to be selective about which additional edge cases to add rather than over-testing.\n\nMy focus should be on fixing the bugs in main.go (type validation order, mode error message, usageError for invalid type), adding a test for filesystem errors like permission denied, and ensuring the existing tests pass against the requirements.\n\nI need to add a test case for permission denied since that's not currently covered, and I should also verify the invalid type test case works correctly after my fixes—the error message should match GNU mknod's output without the \"Try\" suggestion.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the changes: updating main.go to fix the type validation logic, removing the outdated comment about requiring MAJOR MINOR for non-FIFO types, and adding the new test cases to verify the behavior matches the reference implementation. I'm thinking through which test cases would be most valuable to add for the mknod function. The permission denied scenario when creating a FIFO in a read-only directory would cover filesystem errors, and completing the invalid type coverage with full arguments would round out the R2.1 requirements. I should keep the additions focused rather than adding too many tests at once.\n\nLooking back at the actual requirements — R2.1 needs exit 0 on success, R2.2 needs exit 1 on failure or invalid arguments, and R2.3 requires graceful SIGPIPE handling — these are straightforward to verify. The success case is already tested, so I'll focus on adding the permission denied test and then run through the full test suite.\n\nBefore I proceed with the implementation, I want to verify the exact error message format that GNU mknod uses when given an invalid mode like `bogus`, since that affects how I should structure the validation logic. I'm noticing that `mknod` uses `quotearg_colon` which adds quotes around the argument, so the error message becomes `mknod: invalid mode 'bogus'` without an extra colon. But `chmod` actually includes a colon in its format string itself — `\"invalid mode: %s\"` — which is a different pattern. I see the issue now — the error message is being constructed correctly in `parseMode`, but then `run()` is discarding it and printing a generic message instead. I need to update `run()` to actually use the error details when printing to stderr, so it outputs the full error message like `mknod: invalid mode 'bogus'` rather than just `mknod: invalid mode`. Let me verify this matches the GNU behavior by running the tests after making the fix.\n\nNow I'll proceed with the edits: fixing the type validation order in `parseArgs`, changing the invalid device type error handling, updating `run()` to print the actual error message, adding a permission denied test case, and then running the test suite to confirm everything works.\n\nMaking the edits now...","signature":""}]